    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "01\nMake the caller supply the limits\nLocal defaults + per-consumer knobs\n→\nRequired policy inputs\nOutput capture, spooling, framing, resources, lifecycle and diagnostics read the existing snapshot. The operational implementations stay in apps/cli.\nReason: Omitted composition must not recreate local defaults.\nstated",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-required",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "02\nKeep the operating envelope\nExisting values, formulas and untimed waits\n→\nSame values, one policy definition\nDefaults are retained. Memory limits still depend on host memory. No user tuning is added. Healthy startup, accepted completion and worker output acknowledgement still have no deadline; startup silence and result eviction remain deferred.\nReason: The architecture spec requires behavior preservation, one policy owner and no user tuning.\nstated",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-values",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "03\nLet composition choose the response clock\nStatus action supplies 100 ms directly\n→\nStatus-observer purpose selects 100 ms\nThe purpose belongs to a transport instance, not to a message kind. Ordinary lifecycle and execution-status exchanges keep 250 ms; execute admission keeps its separate 5 s clock.\nReason: Status observation and ordinary execution-status requests have distinct deadlines.\nstated",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-purpose",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "04\nCount attempts in their own scopes\nOne-shot reattach / resume / startup retry\n→\nThree numeric limits, all default to 1\nReattachment spans accepted execution; fetch-resume state belongs to each execute attempt, as it already did in base. Startup retry is separate and counts child exit or lost warm-up.\nReason: Each reattached execute attempt needs its own fetch-resume allowance; startup retains a fresh launch after failure.\nstated",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-budgets",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "05\nExpose the reattached completion's error\nRecovery failure rethrows first accepted-close error\n→\nLater completion can expose its own error\nAfter an accepted close, a corrupt completion on the reattachment reaches the caller as corrupt instead of closed. Failure to obtain the reattached receipt still preserves the preceding error.\nunexplained",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-error",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "06\nUse different input shapes at different depths\nDefaults hidden in consumers and helpers\n→\nSnapshot → sections → fields → numbers\nSome consumers take several sections, others one field or a bare number. The worker parent reparses the snapshot for a chunk limit; stored output keeps an optional internal cap. Transport keeps writeChunkSize and outputDirectory options.\nunexplained",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-shapes",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "07\nKeep old tuning calls in test adapters\nTests tune production constructors\n→\nSeven helper files translate into policies\nTest-only wrappers preserve legacy calls and construct validated snapshots. Existing validation now governs their overrides: ordered output and memory limits, integers and positive polling intervals.\nReason: Tests need small limits without restoring production tuning seams.\nstated",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-adapters",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "08\nMake wrappers look like the former APIs\nProduction classes at test import sites\n→\nAdapters aliased to production class names\nTests often keep names such as LocalDaemonTransport while importing TestLocalDaemonTransport. Most helper defaults come from currentSystem(); the workspace helper starts from its supplied or dependency policy.\nunexplained",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-aliases",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "09\nLet adapters rewrite the requested test limits\nInline 0 means spill immediately\n→\nClient inline floor becomes 64 KiB\nThe client helper also creates the output directory. The daemon helper may turn inline 0 into half the result limit, lower chunk/result limits, and ignore memoryCapBytes. Existing cleanup assertions can now run without a spill.\nunexplained",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-rewrite",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "10\nReshape test payloads around valid policy limits\nTiny or zero standalone limits\n→\nCoordinated chunk / inline / result values\ncapture(1) becomes capture(32); one two-byte record becomes two one-byte records. Spool tests raise inline limits and sometimes append an extra record. The aggregate-capacity test now keeps its first completion inline.\nunexplained",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-fixtures",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "11\nMove the resource-policy test surface\nCLI memory recipe + three test definitions\n→\nExisting package policy tests + consumer tests\nThe duplicate CLI recipe and its derivation/constraint/default test blocks are deleted. Existing daemon-package tests cover those subjects. New tests exercise custom capacities, cadence, status purpose, startup retry counts and reattachment scope.\nunexplained",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-tests",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "12\nDelete a startup deadline input that was unused\nstartupTimeoutMs stored but never read\n→\nField and test arguments removed\nRemoving this input does not remove an active startup timer. The policy record already says healthy startup has no deadline; coordination grace remains a different, active clock.\nunexplained",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-dead",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "13\nGuard the retired names in production source\nMeta-test checks three policy bypass strings\n→\nMeta-test checks 36 retired strings\nThe guard scans CLI production source and excludes tests. It prevents the named defaults and options from returning; it does not analyze arbitrary numeric literals or equivalently renamed options.\nunexplained",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#decision-fence",
      "type": null
    },
